A||||7||RNGCRSH||speaker replacement adapters.||||1||
Z||000000||RNGCRSH||01-16-2001||01:45 AM||||I have noticed alot of people putting a midbass and a tweeter in place of the 4x10 in the dash. <P>What is the most common speaker setup for this aplication?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||01-16-2001||02:20 AM||||I used a set of Polk Audio EX Series II with a 4" woofer in mine. 5 1/4" can be put in with minor dash modification and a 6 1/2 with lots of dash modification. So pick a set you like and go for it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/speaker1.jpg"> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMaster88||01-16-2001||02:23 AM||||I went to the Sound shop where i got my amp, sub and deck from.  My salesman said he would love to do 2 Eclipse 4" coaxial speakers on each side of the dash, so 4 total, then a 4" component set in the rears.  He said he saw someone else do that with aonther car, cant remeber what car now, and it sounded great.  That will be a summer project for me!!! <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster86<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles<BR>84 Indy Fiero (restoration Project)||||reg||6
Z||000003||Eric_PA||01-16-2001||12:53 PM||||Have you guys seen the Boston Acustik 4x6 plates. They sell for about $120 and sound great. I am going to be buying a pair for my rear. it is a component set up in an allready made plate with a tweeter and I think it was a 4" speaker, possibly a 3.5" not sure, but was really impressed with the sound.<P>Eric<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000004||Oreif||01-16-2001||01:04 PM||||The Boston's are a 3.5" woofer. They are just like the Infinity Kappa's. I think the Bostons have a better sounding mid-range. I went with a Polk Audio 4" and seperate tweeter for the back.(actually front and back) That way I can get a better mid-bass on the rears. 3.5's can only go down to about 125hz (distortion free) but a 4" can go to 90hz and I'm crossing my sub at 100hz/18db rolloff.<P>Make sure you use a bass block so the plates don't get distortion.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||01-16-2001||06:54 PM||||I've got two Pioneers in each location, making it a total of 4 in the front.  They are Pioneer TS-G1045 4"ers.  They've got an IMPP composite cone and a 1 1/8" cone tweeter.  They add a little to the sound, but not too much.  Better than the crappy 4x10s I had originally.  I will probably still get an 8" sub for a floor enclosure, and maybe a 5.25" for the center console.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000006||webbee||01-16-2001||07:19 PM||||Are those 3.5/4" better sounding than the Pioneer 4/10's?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||01-16-2001||08:48 PM||||In my opinion, any round speaker is acoustically better than any oval speaker.<BR>I have heard many cars with 4" speakers in the front as opposed to 4X10's. The mids and highs are more defined. But a subwoofer is required since a 4" by itself will not produce the lower frequencies like a 4X10.<BR>A 4" can't go much below 90hz efficently (sp?) where a 4X10 can go down to 50hz.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
